.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO_ASYNC 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do_async \- submit an HTTP request for asynchronous execution
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ctpool_future *chttpclient_do_async(chttpcli cli,
                                    const chttp_request_t *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This is the counterpart of chttpclient_do that does not block. It
puts req into the queue of the shared reactor engine of chttpclient,
and it returns at once. The library starts that engine only when it
first needs it. The whole cycle of the request and the response runs
on the threads of the engine. This includes every redirect hop.

The engine holds a small pool of reactor threads and a companion
worker pool for DNS and for connect. The library sizes both to the CPU
count. The engine starts on the first call to chttpclient_do_async or
to chttpclient_do_async_streaming anywhere in the process. It stops on
its own when no request is in flight and no connection is left in the
async idle pool of any chttpcli. That stop runs on a thread of its
own. The reason is that the last reference often goes away inside a
reactor callback. The teardown joins the very threads that run such
a callback. The process can also fail to give that thread,
because of a ceiling on threads or on processes such as RLIMIT_NPROC
or a cgroup pids.max. The engine then stays up, and the library tries
the stop again every 100 ms until a thread is available. A call in the
meantime reuses the engine.

There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with this engine:
  - Call fork(2) before you create a client. Each process then makes
    its own clients and, on first use, its own engine.
  - Call fork(2), and then call exec(3) in the child immediately, as
    popen and system do.
A child that continues with no exec(3) while a client from before the
fork is live is not supported. The reason is that fork(2) does not copy
the threads of the engine.

A process can destroy all clients that used Tier 2 or Tier 3 before it
forks. The child of that process can then use both tiers. The child
starts its own engine on first use. This is also true when the stop of
the engine of the parent was not complete at the time of the fork. See
chttpclient(7).

The engine is fully separate from the synchronous
connection work of chttpclient_do. It is also independent of the
reactor of chttpserver, which sits on ccol_event_loop. One process can
therefore run chttpserver together with chttpclient_do_async and
chttpclient_do_async_streaming. A stop of one engine has no effect on
the other engine. This holds for chttpsvr_engine_stop() and for the
automatic teardown of this engine.

req does not need to stay valid after this call returns, and
chttpclient_do is different in this respect. The library copies or
serializes everything that it needs before this call returns.

The server can send an interim informational response with a 1xx
status. The client drops every such response and reads on, on the same
connection, for the real response. The library therefore never
fulfills the future with a 1xx status as the final answer. "103 Early
Hints" of RFC 8297 is the most common example in the real world. "101
Switching Protocols" is the exception: this client never asks for an
upgrade, so a 101 fulfills the future with ccol_http_transfer_aborted,
as in chttpclient_do. A response that arrives in the same read as a
"100 Continue" is read before any byte of the body goes out, as in
chttpclient_do(3). A
request with req->expect_continue set sends "Expect: 100-continue".
See the doc comment of chttp_request_t. Such a request holds the body
back until the interim response of the server, exactly as in
chttpclient_do. The wait is part of the dispatch of this function,
which does not block, and not a call that blocks. The client does not
accept an endless stream of interim responses. After it drops 64
interim responses in a row on one hop, the library fulfills the future
with ccol_http_transfer_aborted instead of a wait without an end. This
cap applies to two phases on their own. The first phase is the wait
for "100 Continue". The second phase is the read of the final response
after it. A "100 Continue" is a real outcome, and so is the end of the
wait with no response. Neither is a dropped response, and each one
sets the count back to zero. A hop that uses req->expect_continue can
therefore drop up to 64 interim responses during the wait for "100
Continue". It can then drop up to 64 more during the read of the final
response. The total is not 64.

Two more cases are different. The first is a request header with a CR
byte or an LF byte inside it. The same holds for body.content_type
on a request with a body. The second is a request that carries a body
(POST, PUT or PATCH) and whose Content-Length header from the caller
holds a bad value. A good value is a plain unsigned string of decimal
digits. It has no leading '+' or '-', and no whitespace inside it. It
must also match the real length of the body. The library can find
these two problems only when it serializes the request for the wire,
and that happens after the future already exists. It checks a
malformed URL and a TLS configuration that it cannot use up front
instead. Such a request still gives a future that is not NULL. The
result of that future holds rv == ccol_invalid_args instead of a good
response. See chttpclient_async_result_get(3). chttpclient_do gives
the same code synchronously for the equivalent request. See
chttp_request_set_header(3) and chttpclient_do(3) for the reason that
the client rejects this.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
req The request to run. It must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A future, or NULL if the library cannot even queue the request. The
library gives NULL for a req that is NULL. It gives NULL for a cli
that is CHTTPCLI_INVALID, and for a stale cli of a client that is
already destroyed. It also gives NULL for a malformed URL and for
TLS that it cannot use. It gives NULL when it is out of memory, and
when the engine fails to start. After a success, the caller owns the
future. The caller must call chttpclient_async_result_get, then
chttpclient_async_result_free, and then exactly one
ctpool_future_free.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library leaves the disposition of SIGPIPE to the application.  It
never changes it, in any tier.  No write of the client raises that
signal: every write to a socket, a TLS record and the close_notify of
a TLS teardown included, is a send(2) with MSG_NOSIGNAL.  A server that
closes or resets the connection while the client writes a request
therefore fails that request, and never ends the process.

The engine stops its threads before the process exits. A handler that
the library registers with atexit(3), after the one of OpenSSL so that
it runs first, waits for a stop in progress, destroys the default
client when no call runs on it, and stops an engine that nothing holds
any more. No thread of the engine is then left to race the cleanup of
OpenSSL. Two cases keep the engine running at exit, and the handler
then does nothing: a request still in flight, which includes an exit()
from inside a write_fn, and a client that the application never
destroyed, whose pooled connections hold the engine. Destroy every
client before the process returns from main.
.fi
